					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/vaelynx" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(914331); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=914331&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607460491" alt="VAElynx" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=914331)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/vaelynx" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(914331); return false;" >VAElynx</a></span> <span class="odate time_1410172510 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Sep 2014 10:35</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2104956">
									

<p>Literal dust bunnies leave me unimpressed (I'd have expected a little more exploration of the concept - given they can easily escape natural predators, what exactly has been limiting their population up to now? Myxomatosis?) &#8230; and what's with that huge containment enclosure with a three metre roof, to boot?</p>
<p>In addition to being inherently a stupid waste of space, in this specific case it makes the personnel's work harder since the containment procedure also involves the removal of all the rabbits from the containment chamber, daily. (Because, guess what, a rabbit will see you catching it or trying to as a 'possible threat' most of the time) I completely do not envy the personnel in charge of <em>that</em>, never mind, it seems like a pointless provision, as ultimately is the daily cleaning.</p>
<p>-1, which is a shame since anomalous biology is one of my sweet spots.</p>

							</div>

								
					
