<p>Uh, yes I did?<br />
While it has a grain of a good idea there, ultimately it's not enough for me to look at this in a positive light.</p>
<p>I wonder though - it can only transform into a dead rabbit once it dies? What does it accomplish by this - certainly not making more of themselves since that's what living matter does.</p>
<p>Unless&#8230;<br />
This is rabbit refuse that has, as its preferred state, being a dead rabbit. To this extent it transforms into a live rabbit, who then seeks to die, after fulfilling its basic biologic needs, perhaps including reproduction.</p>
<p>This is the real reason they haven't overpopulated - their rabbit-form's selfpreservation instinct is countered by the inanimate's form desire to become a dead rabbit, which leads to periods of erratic behaviour where they seek death.</p>
<p>So why do they escape from predators? Because they do not want to become <em>eaten</em> rabbits. Just a natural, dessicated corpse. Something like those walled-in buddhist monk mummies.</p>
<p>Who knows, there actually might be a core of a good idea here.</p>

