			<div class="content" id="post-content-2105272">
									

<p>I read it, and probably would have downvoted for that on its own. I intensely dislike having in-universe researchers pontificate in a clinical document, and I especially dislike it when it's used to directly tell the reader what's going on in the article. In about 99% of cases, I find it to be lazy storytelling. And beyond that, why exactly is the idea that it's &quot;dust that turns into rabbits sometimes&quot; so much more compelling than &quot;rabbits that turn into dust sometimes&quot;?</p>
<p>In any event, this article is a D&amp;D monster manual entry predicated on a joke, with filler logs that don't really tell us anything we wouldn't immediately know from the main article description and a completely immersion-breaking &quot;Foundation agent happens to spot anomaly out in the wild at random&quot; recovery entry.</p>
<p>Parenthetically to all of this, I also can't say I'm a fan of &quot;well, you must not be getting it then&quot; as an authorial reaction to criticism.</p>

